    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "### Describe the bug\n\nA compiled coroutine handles an exception and awaits a pure-Python coroutine. That coroutine suspends, then re-raises the same exception. When this happens, `__Pyx_Coroutine_SendEx` finds `f_back` already set on the top frame of the exception's traceback.\n\nThat top frame belongs to the finished Python coroutine. CPython set its `f_back` to the calling Python frame because the frame outlived its call (`take_ownership()` in `Python/frame.c`). Cython then resumes the compiled coroutine to propagate the exception, and `__Pyx_Coroutine_SendEx` assumes `f_back` is NULL:\n\n- With `-DNDEBUG` (which is in Python's default extension CFLAGS), the assert is compiled out. `f_back` is overwritten without releasing the old reference, so a frame leaks on every call (the caller's frame in the example below).\n- Without `-DNDEBUG`, the process aborts:\n\n```\n__Pyx_Coroutine_SendEx: Assertion `f->f_back == NULL' failed.\n```\n\nThe same code in pure Python does not leak.\n\n### Code to reproduce the behaviour:\n\n`work.py` (compiled with Cython):\n\n```python\nimport helper\n\n\nasync def work():\n    try:\n        raise ValueError(\"x\")\n    except ValueError as e:\n        await helper.reraise(e)\n```\n\n`helper.py` (pure Python):\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\n\n\nasync def reraise(e):\n    await asyncio.sleep(0)\n    raise e\n```\n\n`leak_test.py` (pure Python driver; the argument is the folder to import `work` from):\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nimport gc\nimport sys \nimport types\n\nsys.path.insert(0, sys.argv[1])\nimport work\n\n\nasync def caller():\n    try:\n        await work.work()\n    except ValueError:\n        pass\n\n\nasync def run(n):\n    for _ in range(n):\n        await caller()\n\n\ndef frames():\n    gc.collect()\n    return sum(isinstance(o, types.FrameType) for o in gc.get_objects())\n\n\nasyncio.run(run(10))\nbefore = frames()\nasyncio.run(run(1000))\nprint(f\"{work.__file__.rsplit('/', 1)[-1]}: frames after 10 runs: {before}, after 1010 runs: {frames()}\")\n```\n\nCommands:\n\n```\nmkdir -p build\ncython -3 work.py -o build/work.c\ngcc -O2 -DNDEBUG -shared -fPIC $(python3-config --includes) build/work.c -o build/work$(python3-config --extension-suffix)\npython3 leak_test.py build\npython3 leak_test.py .\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```\nwork.cpython-312-x86_64-linux-gnu.so: frames after 10 runs: 17, after 1010 runs: 1024\nwork.py: frames after 10 runs: 0, after 1010 runs: 0\n```\n\nBuilt without `-DNDEBUG`, `python3 leak_test.py build` aborts with the assertion above.\n\nWe first hit this when cancelling a task inside an `@asynccontextmanager` that wraps `async with TaskGroup()`. `TaskGroup.__aexit__` awaits, then re-raises the `CancelledError`:\n\n<details>\n<summary>TaskGroup example (67 -> 6074 frames)</summary>\n\n`ctx.py` (compiled with Cython):\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom asyncio import TaskGroup\nfrom contextlib import asynccontextmanager\n\n\nasync def forever():\n    await asyncio.sleep(3600)\n\n\n@asynccontextmanager\nasync def managed():\n    try:\n        async with TaskGroup() as tg:\n            task = tg.create_task(forever())\n            yield\n            task.cancel()\n    finally:\n        await asyncio.sleep(0)\n```\n\n`leak_test_ctx.py` (pure Python driver):\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nimport gc\nimport sys\nimport types\n\nsys.path.insert(0, sys.argv[1])\nimport ctx\n\n\nasync def user():\n    async with ctx.managed():\n        try:\n            await asyncio.sleep(3600)\n        except asyncio.CancelledError:\n            await asyncio.sleep(0)\n            raise\n\n\nasync def once():\n    task = asyncio.create_task(user())\n    for _ in range(3):\n        await asyncio.sleep(0)\n    task.cancel()\n    try:\n        await task\n    except BaseException:\n        pass\n\n\ndef frames():\n    gc.collect()\n    return sum(isinstance(o, types.FrameType) for o in gc.get_objects())\n\n\nasync def run(n):\n    for _ in range(n):\n        await once()\n\n\nasyncio.run(run(10))\nbefore = frames()\nasyncio.run(run(1000))\nprint(f\"{ctx.__file__.rsplit('/', 1)[-1]}: frames after 10 runs: {before}, after 1010 runs: {frames()}\")\n```\n\n```\nctx.cpython-312-x86_64-linux-gnu.so: frames after 10 runs: 67, after 1010 runs: 6074\nctx.py: frames after 10 runs: 0, after 1010 runs: 0\n```\n\n</details>\n\n### Expected behaviour\n\nNo frames leak (same as pure Python), and no assertion failure.\n\n### OS\n\nUbuntu 24.04, x86_64, gcc 13.3\n\n### Python version\n\n3.12.3\n\n### Cython version\n\n3.3.0 (3.2.9 behaves the same; master still has the same code in Cython/Utility/Coroutine.c)\n\n### Additional context\n\nReleasing the old `f_back` reference when overwriting it fixes the leak in both examples. The resulting `f_back` is the same as now; in the first example, the old and new values are the same frame. This is the change I tested:\n\n```diff\n--- a/Cython/Utility/Coroutine.c\n+++ b/Cython/Utility/Coroutine.c\n@@ -848,7 +848,8 @@\n             PyTracebackObject *tb = (PyTracebackObject *) exc_tb;\n             PyFrameObject *f = tb->tb_frame;\n \n-            assert(f->f_back == NULL);\n+            // f_back may already be set: CPython sets it on finished Python frames kept by the traceback.\n+            PyFrameObject *old_f_back = f->f_back;\n             #if PY_VERSION_HEX >= 0x030B00A1\n             // PyThreadState_GetFrame returns NULL if there isn't a current frame\n             // which is a valid state so no need to check\n@@ -857,6 +858,7 @@\n             Py_XINCREF(tstate->frame);\n             f->f_back = tstate->frame;\n             #endif\n+            Py_XDECREF(old_f_back);\n             #if PY_VERSION_HEX >= 0x030B00a4 && !CYTHON_COMPILING_IN_CPYTHON\n             Py_DECREF(exc_tb);\n             #endif\n```\n\nPossibly related: #4207",
